## sn22.53
sn22.53:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn22.53:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, if you get involved, you’re not free. If you’re not involved, you’re free. | T: “Bhikkhus, one who is engaged is unliberated; one who is disengaged is liberated.
sn22.53:1.3 | S: Should consciousness involved with form, supported by form, established on form continue to remain, with a sprinkling of relishing it would grow, increase, and mature. | T: Consciousness, bhikkhus, while standing, might stand engaged with form; based upon form, established upon form, with a sprinkling of delight, it might come to growth, increase, and expansion.
sn22.53:1.4 | S: Or should consciousness involved with feeling remain … | T: Or consciousness, while standing, might stand engaged with feeling …
sn22.53:1.5 | S: Or should consciousness involved with perception remain … | T: engaged with perception …
sn22.53:1.6 | S: Or should consciousness involved with choices, supported by choices, established on choices continue to remain, with a sprinkling of relishing it would grow, increase, and mature. | T: engaged with volitional formations; based upon volitional formations, established upon volitional formations, with a sprinkling of delight, it might come to growth, increase, and expansion.
sn22.53:2.1 | S: Mendicants, suppose you say: | T: “Bhikkhus, though someone might say:
sn22.53:2.2 | S: ‘Apart from form, feeling, perception, and choices, I will describe the coming and going of consciousness, its passing away and reappearing, its growth, increase, and maturity.’ That is not possible. | T: ‘Apart from form, apart from feeling, apart from perception, apart from volitional formations, I will make known the coming and going of consciousness, its passing away and rebirth, its growth, increase, and expansion’—that is impossible.
sn22.53:3.1 | S: If a mendicant has given up greed for the form element, | T: “Bhikkhus, if a bhikkhu has abandoned lust for the form element,
sn22.53:3.2 | S: the support is cut off, and there is no establishment of consciousness. | T: with the abandoning of lust the basis is cut off: there is no support for the establishing of consciousness.
sn22.53:3.3 | S: If a mendicant has given up greed for the feeling element … | T: If he has abandoned lust for the feeling element …
sn22.53:3.4 | S: perception element … | T: for the perception element …
sn22.53:3.5 | S: choices element … | T: for the volitional formations element …
sn22.53:3.6 | S: consciousness element, | T: for the consciousness element,
sn22.53:3.7 | S: the support is cut off, and there is no establishment of consciousness. | T: with the abandoning of lust the basis is cut off: there is no support for the establishing of consciousness.
sn22.53:3.8 | S: Since that consciousness does not become established and does not grow, not making choices, it is freed. | T: “When that consciousness is unestablished, not coming to growth, nongenerative, it is liberated.
sn22.53:3.9 | S: Being free, it’s stable. Being stable, it’s content. Being content, they’re not anxious. Not being anxious, they personally become extinguished. | T: By being liberated, it is steady; by being steady, it is content; by being content, he is not agitated. Being unagitated, he personally attains Nibbāna.
sn22.53:3.10 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’” | T: He understands: ‘Destroyed is birth, the holy life has been lived, what had to be done has been done, there is no more for this state of being.’”
sn22.53:3.11 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn22.54
sn22.54:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn22.54:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, there are five kinds of plants. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these five kinds of seeds.
sn22.54:1.3 | S: What five? | T: What five?
sn22.54:1.4 | S: Plants propagated from roots, trunks, cuttings, or joints; and those from regular seeds are the fifth. | T: Root-seeds, stem-seeds, joint-seeds, cutting-seeds, and germ-seeds as the fifth.
sn22.54:1.5 | S: Suppose these five kinds of plants were intact, unspoiled, not weather-damaged, fertile, and well-kept. But there’s no earth or water. | T: If these five kinds of seeds are unbroken, unspoilt, undamaged by wind and sun, fertile, securely planted, but there is no earth or water, would these five kinds of seeds come to growth, increase, and expansion?” “No, venerable sir.” “If these five kinds of seeds are broken, spoilt, damaged by wind and sun, unfertile, not securely planted, but there is earth and water,
sn22.54:1.6 | S: Then would these five kinds of plants reach growth, increase, and maturity?” | T: would these five kinds of seeds come to growth, increase, and expansion?”
sn22.54:1.7 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, venerable sir.”
sn22.54:1.8 | S: “Suppose these five kinds of plants were intact, unspoiled, not weather-damaged, fertile, and well-kept. And there is earth and water. | T: “If these five kinds of seeds are unbroken, unspoilt, undamaged by wind and sun, fertile, securely planted, and there is earth and water,
sn22.54:1.9 | S: Then would these five kinds of plants reach growth, increase, and maturity?” | T: would these five kinds of seeds come to growth, increase, and expansion?”
sn22.54:1.10 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, venerable sir.”
sn22.54:1.11 | S: “The four establishings of consciousness should be seen as like the earth element. | T: “Bhikkhus, the four stations of consciousness should be seen as like the earth element.
sn22.54:1.12 | S: Relishing and greed should be seen as like the water element. | T: Delight and lust should be seen as like the water element.
sn22.54:1.13 | S: Consciousness with its fuel should be seen as like the five kinds of plants. | T: Consciousness together with its nutriment should be seen as like the five kinds of seeds.
sn22.54:2.1 | S: Should consciousness involved with form, supported by form, established on form continue to remain, with a sprinkling of relishing it would grow, increase, and mature. | T: “Consciousness, bhikkhus, while standing, might stand engaged with form; based upon form, established upon form, with a sprinkling of delight, it might come to growth, increase, and expansion.
sn22.54:2.2 | S: Or should consciousness involved with feeling remain … | T: Or consciousness, while standing, might stand engaged with feeling …
sn22.54:2.3 | S: Or should consciousness involved with perception remain … | T: engaged with perception …
sn22.54:2.4 | S: Or should consciousness involved with choices, supported by choices, established on choices continue to remain, with a sprinkling of relishing it would grow, increase, and mature. | T: engaged with volitional formations; based upon volitional formations, established upon volitional formations, with a sprinkling of delight, it might come to growth, increase, and expansion.
sn22.54:3.1 | S: Mendicants, suppose you say: | T: “Bhikkhus, though someone might say:
sn22.54:3.2 | S: ‘Apart from form, feeling, perception, and choices, I will describe the coming and going of consciousness, its passing away and reappearing, its growth, increase, and maturity.’ That is not possible. | T: ‘Apart from form, apart from feeling, apart from perception, apart from volitional formations, I will make known the coming and going of consciousness, its passing away and rebirth, its growth, increase, and expansion’—that is impossible.
sn22.54:4.1 | S: If a mendicant has given up greed for the form element, | T: “Bhikkhus, if a bhikkhu has abandoned lust for the form element,
sn22.54:4.2 | S: the support is cut off, and there is no establishment of consciousness. | T: with the abandoning of lust the basis is cut off: there is no support for the establishing of consciousness.
sn22.54:4.3 | S: If a mendicant has given up greed for the feeling element … | T: If he has abandoned lust for the feeling element …
sn22.54:4.4 | S: perception element … | T: for the perception element …
sn22.54:4.5 | S: choices element … | T: for the volitional formations element …
sn22.54:4.6 | S: consciousness element, | T: for the consciousness element,
sn22.54:4.7 | S: the support is cut off, and there is no establishment of consciousness. | T: with the abandoning of lust the basis is cut off: there is no support for the establishing of consciousness.
sn22.54:4.8 | S: Since that consciousness does not become established and does not grow, not making choices, it is freed. | T: “When that consciousness is unestablished, not coming to growth, nongenerative, it is liberated.
sn22.54:4.9 | S: Being free, it’s stable. Being stable, it’s content. Being content, they’re not anxious. Not being anxious, they personally become extinguished. | T: By being liberated, it is steady; by being steady, it is content; by being content, he is not agitated. Being unagitated, he personally attains Nibbāna.
sn22.54:4.10 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended … there is nothing further for this place.’” | T: He understands: ‘Destroyed is birth, the holy life has been lived, what had to be done has been done, there is no more for this state of being.’”
sn22.54:4.11 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn22.56
sn22.56:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn22.56:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, there are these five grasping aggregates. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these five aggregates subject to clinging.
sn22.56:1.3 | S: What five? | T: What five?
sn22.56:1.4 | S: The grasping aggregates of form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness. | T: The form aggregate subject to clinging, the feeling aggregate subject to clinging, the perception aggregate subject to clinging, the volitional formations aggregate subject to clinging, the consciousness aggregate subject to clinging.
sn22.56:1.5 | S: As long as I didn’t truly understand these five grasping aggregates in four rounds, I didn’t announce my supreme perfect awakening in this world with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans. | T: “So long as I did not directly know as they really are the five aggregates subject to clinging in four phases, I did not claim to have awakened to the unsurpassed perfect enlightenment in this world with its devas, Mara, and Brahma, in this generation with its ascetics and brahmins, its devas and humans.
sn22.56:1.6 | S: But when I did truly understand these five grasping aggregates in four rounds, I announced my supreme perfect awakening in this world with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans. | T: But when I directly knew all this as it really is, then I claimed to have awakened to the unsurpassed perfect enlightenment in this world with … its devas and humans.
sn22.56:1.7 | P: sadevamanussāya anuttaraṁ sammāsambodhiṁ abhisambuddhoti paccaññāsiṁ. | T: (none)
sn22.56:2.1 | S: And how are there four rounds? | T: “And how, bhikkhus, are there four phases?
sn22.56:2.2 | S: I directly knew form, its origin, its cessation, and the practice that leads to its cessation. | T: I directly knew form, its origin, its cessation, and the way leading to its cessation.

