sn22.57:14.1 | S: And what is consciousness? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is consciousness?
sn22.57:14.2 | S: There are these six classes of consciousness: | T: There are these six classes of consciousness:
sn22.57:14.3 | S: eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind consciousness. | T: eye-consciousness … mind-consciousness.
sn22.57:14.4 | S: This is called consciousness. | T: This is called consciousness.
sn22.57:14.5 | S: Consciousness originates from name and form. | T: With the arising of name-and-form there is the arising of consciousness.
sn22.57:14.6 | S: When name and form cease, consciousness ceases. | T: With the cessation of name-and-form there is the cessation of consciousness.
sn22.57:14.7 | S: The practice that leads to the cessation of consciousness is simply this noble eightfold path, that is: | T: This Noble Eightfold Path is the way leading to the cessation of consciousness; that is,
sn22.57:14.8 | S: right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: right view … right concentration.
sn22.57:15.1 | S: The pleasure and happiness that arise from consciousness: this is its gratification. | T: “The pleasure and joy that arise in dependence on consciousness: this is the gratification in consciousness.
sn22.57:15.2 | P: ayaṁ viññāṇassa assādo. | T: (none)
sn22.57:15.3 | S: That consciousness is impermanent, suffering, and perishable: this is its drawback. | T: That consciousness is impermanent, suffering, and subject to change: this is the danger in consciousness.
sn22.57:15.4 | P: ayaṁ viññāṇassa ādīnavo. | T: (none)
sn22.57:15.5 | S: Removing and giving up desire and greed for consciousness: this is its escape. | T: The removal and abandonment of desire and lust for consciousness: this is the escape from consciousness.
sn22.57:15.6 | P: idaṁ viññāṇassa nissaraṇaṁ. | T: (none)
sn22.57:16.1 | S: Those ascetics and brahmins who have directly known consciousness in this way—and its origin, its cessation, and the practice that leads to its cessation; | T: “Whatever ascetics and brahmins, having thus directly known consciousness, its origin, its cessation, and the way leading to its cessation,
sn22.57:16.2 | S: its gratification, drawback, and escape—and are practicing for disillusionment, dispassion, and cessation regarding consciousness: they are practicing well. | T: having thus directly known the gratification, the danger, and the escape in the case of consciousness, are practising for the purpose of revulsion towards consciousness, for its fading away and cessation, they are practising well.
sn22.57:16.3 | S: Those who practice well have a footing  in this teaching and training. | T: Those who are practising well have gained a foothold in this Dhamma and Discipline.
sn22.57:17.1 | S: Those ascetics and brahmins who have directly known consciousness in this way—and its origin, its cessation, and the practice that leads to its cessation; | T: “And whatever ascetics and brahmins, having thus directly known consciousness, its origin, its cessation, and the way leading to its cessation,
sn22.57:17.2 | S: its gratification, drawback, and escape—and due to disillusionment, dispassion, and cessation regarding consciousness, are freed by not grasping: they are well freed. | T: having thus directly known the gratification, the danger, and the escape in the case of consciousness, through revulsion towards consciousness, through its fading away and cessation, are liberated by nonclinging, they are well liberated.
sn22.57:17.3 | S: Those who are well freed are consummate ones. | T: Those who are well liberated are consummate ones.
sn22.57:17.4 | S: For consummate ones, there is no cycle of rebirths to be found. | T: As to those consummate ones, there is no round for describing them.
sn22.57:17.5 | S: That’s how a mendicant is skilled in seven cases. | T: “It is in such a way, bhikkhus, that a bhikkhu is skilled in seven cases.
sn22.57:18.1 | S: And how does a mendicant examine in three ways? | T: “And how, bhikkhus, is a bhikkhu a triple investigator?
sn22.57:18.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant examines by way of the elements, sense fields, and dependent origination. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu investigates by way of the elements, by way of the sense bases, and by way of dependent origination.
sn22.57:18.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant examines in three ways. | T: It is in such a way that a bhikkhu is a triple investigator.
sn22.57:18.4 | S: In this teaching and training, a mendicant who is skilled in seven cases and who examines in three ways is called consummate, accomplished, a supreme person.” | T: “Bhikkhus, a bhikkhu who is skilled in these seven cases and a triple investigator is called, in this Dhamma and Discipline, a consummate one, one who has fully lived the holy life, the highest kind of person.”
sn22.57:18.5 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)


