sn22.54:1.4 | S: Plants propagated from roots, trunks, cuttings, or joints; and those from regular seeds are the fifth. | T: Root-seeds, stem-seeds, joint-seeds, cutting-seeds, and germ-seeds as the fifth.
sn22.54:1.5 | S: Suppose these five kinds of plants were intact, unspoiled, not weather-damaged, fertile, and well-kept. But there’s no earth or water. | T: If these five kinds of seeds are unbroken, unspoilt, undamaged by wind and sun, fertile, securely planted, but there is no earth or water, would these five kinds of seeds come to growth, increase, and expansion?” “No, venerable sir.” “If these five kinds of seeds are broken, spoilt, damaged by wind and sun, unfertile, not securely planted, but there is earth and water,
sn22.54:1.6 | S: Then would these five kinds of plants reach growth, increase, and maturity?” | T: would these five kinds of seeds come to growth, increase, and expansion?”
sn22.54:1.7 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, venerable sir.”
